Myofascial Pain Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Deep localized aching muscle pain, Palpable exquisitely tender trigger points, Predictable referred pain patterns, Normal inflammatory markers/muscle enzymes

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-034
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe left-sided neck and shoulder pain radiating to the head, causing severe restriction of movement and mimicking a cervical radiculopathy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with severe, acute, deep aching pain in the neck and shoulder, associated with an inability to move the cervical spine and severe radiating headaches. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively perform a neurological examination, rule out an acute cervical disc herniation or meningitis, and provide immediate mechanical and pharmacological relief before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My neck is locked completely solid on the left side, and there is a burning, deep ache shooting straight up into the back of my eye."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute exacerbation of chronic, deep, and severe localized aching pain in the left upper trapezius and levator scapulae muscle groups. The pain has caused a severely restricted active and passive range of motion in the cervical spine due to profound muscle tightness. The patient reports prominent stiffness and severe localized fatigue. The severe, unremitting localized pain is causing acute sleep disruption tonight. The patient notes the severe ache shoots into the back of the head when pressing on specific spots on the shoulder. Complete absence of generalized widespread tenderness (unlike fibromyalgia).
Functional Status: Sitting rigidly, holding the head slightly tilted to the left to avoid stretching the musculature.
Social/Safety Concerns: High distress due to pain intensity and complete inability to sleep.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, guarding the left neck and shoulder, moving the entire torso rather than turning the neck.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination of the upper limbs is completely normal (power 5/5, normal reflexes, intact sensation), strictly ruling out cervical radiculopathy. Palpation of the left upper trapezius reveals highly localized, hyperirritable, and exquisitely tender taut bands of skeletal muscle (trigger points). Firm palpation of these active trigger points elicits a distinct, predictable referred pain pattern radiating directly to the left occiput and retro-orbital region. A local twitch response is palpable when the trigger point is snapped. Documented history of completely normal inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP) and normal muscle enzymes (CK).
Environmental Observations: Patient's workstation visible in the background displays a poorly positioned dual-monitor setup requiring constant cervical rotation.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted paracetamol with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Myofascial Pain Syndrome - Status: Acute trigger point flare
Differentials:

Cervical Radiculopathy (Herniated Disc) - Ruled out by the completely normal upper limb neurological exam (no motor, sensory, or reflex deficits) and pain that refers upward rather than down the arm.

Fibromyalgia - Ruled out as the pain and tenderness are strictly localized to a specific regional muscle group, lacking the widespread, generalized tenderness required for fibromyalgia.

Meningitis - Ruled out completely by the absence of fever, photophobia, and lack of true nuchal rigidity (pain is unilateral muscular stiffness, not meningismus).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to severe, acute neck and shoulder pain causing functional immobility and radiating head pain that clinically mimicked an acute cervical nerve root compression or structural spinal pathology. An immediate, targeted neurological and musculoskeletal examination was mandatory to definitively rule out a herniated disc or neurovascular compromise. By specifically identifying the localized muscular trigger points and reproducing the exact referred pain pattern, while confirming completely intact neurology, the physician safely diagnosed a benign mechanical flare. This immediate clinical diagnosis safely prevented a highly distressed, unnecessary presentation to the emergency department for advanced spinal imaging, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed localized ischemic compression (manual trigger point release) on the left upper trapezius, yielding immediate partial relief of the referred headache. Prescribed a short course of oral NSAIDs (Ibuprofen 400mg TDS) and a topical NSAID gel (Diclofenac) to reduce local inflammation at the taut band.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of holding the neck rigidly still to avoid pain, actively perform gentle, sustained stretching of the trapezius muscle immediately after a hot shower. The heat increases local perfusion, and the mechanical stretch forcibly lengthens the contracted sarcomeres in the trigger point, directly rewarding the patient with restored range of motion and permanently breaking the spasm cycle.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a physiotherapist for dry needling and ergonomic assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week if pain persists, to consider targeted trigger point injections.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing sudden true weakness in the arm or hand, numbness or 'pins and needles' radiating down the arm into the fingers, or if the severe neck pain is accompanied by a high fever and vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on normal localized neurology; development of arm numbness or weakness requires prompt reassessment.
Clinical Course: Myofascial pain is caused by a highly localized knot of muscle fibers that have become chemically locked in a contracted state. It causes severe, referred pain but is mechanically benign. Releasing the knot via heat, stretching, or massage cures the pain.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you suddenly develop numbness or weakness traveling down your arm into your hand, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain in your head is a 'referred' signal originating from a tight knot of muscle in your shoulder. Traditional painkillers fail because the muscle is physically locked tight. Manual pressure, heat, and stretching are required to physically squeeze the accumulated inflammatory chemicals out of the knot and force it to relax.
Actionable Steps: Apply a hot wheat bag directly to the hard knot in your shoulder for 20 minutes, then firmly press a tennis ball against a wall right into that knot. The sustained, deep pressure physically forces the contracted muscle fibers to release, immediately stopping the referred pain to your head.

SMS INFORMATION

